WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.983 --> 00:00:05.840
Ah, it's beautiful.

00:00:05.840 --> 00:00:07.610
I remember cooking
my first brisket

00:00:07.610 --> 00:00:09.502
like it was 16 years ago.

00:00:09.502 --> 00:00:10.460
It was pretty terrible.

00:00:10.460 --> 00:00:12.260
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:12.260 --> 00:00:13.820
From the first
brisket in my backyard

00:00:13.820 --> 00:00:18.770
to 106 briskets every day,
I'm also still learning.

00:00:18.770 --> 00:00:20.730
And I want to share
these things with you.

00:00:20.730 --> 00:00:29.310
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:29.310 --> 00:00:32.800
Oh my, this looks really nice.

00:00:32.800 --> 00:00:34.600
So I'm going to
teach you exactly

00:00:34.600 --> 00:00:37.120
like I would train someone
at Franklin Barbecue.

00:00:37.120 --> 00:00:41.015
But I've never really gotten
to do this level of detail.

00:00:41.015 --> 00:00:42.640
That's what I'm
looking for on airflow.

00:00:42.640 --> 00:00:44.513
That smoke is really,
really important.

00:00:44.513 --> 00:00:46.180
That's where that
flavor is coming from.

00:00:46.180 --> 00:00:49.980
Fire is really fun to watch.

00:00:49.980 --> 00:00:51.750
We're going to learn
how to build fires.

00:00:51.750 --> 00:00:54.420
We're going to learn
how to maintain fires.

00:00:54.420 --> 00:00:55.670
We're going to cook pork butt.

00:00:55.670 --> 00:00:57.350
We're going to pull it.

00:00:57.350 --> 00:01:00.620
Steamy pork butts, and guys
with glasses, gee whiz.

00:01:00.620 --> 00:01:01.970
We're going to cook some ribs.

00:01:01.970 --> 00:01:03.720
And I'm going to show
you guys how to cook

00:01:03.720 --> 00:01:05.330
a brisket from start to finish.

00:01:05.330 --> 00:01:08.930
Really get down to the details
of how to trim, how to wrap it,

00:01:08.930 --> 00:01:10.590
and then at the end, slicing it.

00:01:10.590 --> 00:01:11.700
That's very well done.

00:01:11.700 --> 00:01:12.950
I'm proud of this whole thing.

00:01:12.950 --> 00:01:14.533
I should find some
people to eat this.

00:01:14.533 --> 00:01:16.265
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:01:19.060 --> 00:01:19.560
Sharpie.

00:01:19.560 --> 00:01:22.230
In a perfect world, this
brisket would take me 12 hours.

00:01:22.230 --> 00:01:23.030
255.

00:01:23.030 --> 00:01:24.870
About 9 o'clock,
start to ramp up.

00:01:24.870 --> 00:01:25.590
280.

00:01:25.590 --> 00:01:26.970
I work at five-degree
increments.

00:01:26.970 --> 00:01:27.840
Have a plan.

00:01:27.840 --> 00:01:30.780
Be ready to make mistakes, but
also document your mistakes.

00:01:30.780 --> 00:01:32.850
I really like to keep
it super simple--

00:01:32.850 --> 00:01:37.160
kosher salt and 16
mesh black pepper.

00:01:37.160 --> 00:01:39.990
I watched that movie
"Cocktail" a few times.

00:01:39.990 --> 00:01:43.388
It's almost like a typewriter
going back and forth.

00:01:43.388 --> 00:01:45.180
There's a lot of wiggle
room in this stuff.

00:01:45.180 --> 00:01:46.290
Learn the basics.

00:01:46.290 --> 00:01:48.640
But you only learn how
to make good barbecue

00:01:48.640 --> 00:01:52.190
by making bad barbecue.

00:01:52.190 --> 00:01:54.710
My favorite part about
barbecue might actually not

00:01:54.710 --> 00:01:59.637
be eating it, it's the
process and how we get there.

00:01:59.637 --> 00:02:01.720
You cook with your heart,
pour your heart and soul

00:02:01.720 --> 00:02:02.710
into this thing.

00:02:02.710 --> 00:02:04.735
And then at the end,
share that with people.

00:02:04.735 --> 00:02:07.450
That's really the art of
barbecue in a lot of ways.

00:02:07.450 --> 00:02:10.800
I'm Aaron Franklin, and
this is my MasterClass.

00:02:10.800 --> 00:02:15.810
[MUSIC PLAYING]

